{% from "macros.njk" import callout, snippet with context %}

{% set design %}
<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-usage">Usage</h2>
  <p>A menu displays a list of choices on a temporary surface. It appears when
     the user interacts with a button, action or other control — the classic
     trigger is the ⋮ overflow icon on a top bar or a list row. Anchor the
     same panel to a field instead and it becomes the
     <a href="select.html">select</a>: M2 files both under Menus, and Flint
     ships them as one component with two anchors.</p>
  <h3>Do use it for</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Overflow actions</strong> — the secondary actions that don't
        fit on a bar or row (most-used actions stay visible, the rest go in
        the menu).</li>
    <li><strong>Compact choices</strong> — picking one of a few options
        without committing a form row to it.</li>
    <li><strong>Contextual commands</strong> — actions for the item the
        trigger belongs to (duplicate, share, remove).</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Don't use it for</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Blocking decisions</strong> — that is a
        <a href="dialog.html">dialog</a>; menus never demand an answer.</li>
    <li><strong>Long or grouped content</strong> — settings panes, filters
        with many fields. That is a <a href="bottom-sheet.html">bottom
        sheet</a> — M2 itself names it the mobile replacement for inline
        menus.</li>
    <li><strong>Selection inside a form</strong> — a form-bound choice is a
        <a href="select.html">select</a>; that covers M2's <em>exposed
        dropdown menu</em> (the variation that shows the current selection
        above the list).</li>
  </ul>
  {{ callout({
    variant: "info",
    title: "The hard parts are platform work — by design",
    text: "The menu is a native popover: tap-outside dismiss, Escape, one-menu-at-a-time and top-layer stacking come from the browser. No scrim (M2 menus aren't modal — they simply render above everything), no z-index juggling. flint.js adds only anchoring, aria-expanded sync and the arrow-key pattern.",
    href: "dialog.html",
    linkText: "Dialog"
  }) }}
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-types">Types</h2>
  <table class="docs-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Type</th><th>Shape</th><th>Use it for</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Dropdown menu</strong></td>
        <td>Anchor + <code>popover</code> panel; rows are
            <code>fl-option</code> buttons with <code>role="menuitem"</code>
        </td>
        <td>The ⋮ overflow — secondary actions on bars, cards and list rows</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Text + icon list</strong></td>
        <td>Rows with a leading icon slot; optional
            <code>fl-option__meta</code> for keyboard commands (⌘X)</td>
        <td>Row and card menus where icons aid scanning (M2 anatomy)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Selection-state list</strong></td>
        <td>Rows as <code>role="menuitemradio"</code> with
            <code>aria-checked</code>; the check glyph draws in CSS, all
            radio rows share a leading gutter</td>
        <td>Single-choice menus (line spacing, sort order)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Exposed dropdown</strong></td>
        <td colspan="2"><em>Not this component</em> — M2's variation that
            displays the current selection above the list is the
            <a href="select.html">select</a> in Flint.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><strong>Deliberately not in Flint:</strong> cascading (sub)menus —
     desktop-only in M2 and a nesting depth apps rarely need; a second level
     is better modeled as an item that opens a dialog or navigates. Dense
     menus follow the same rule as the top bar's dense variants.</p>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-anatomy">Anatomy</h2>
  <p>M2's text list, text-and-icon list, and text-with-selection-state list
     are all one structure — the panel plus the shared
     <a href="dialog.html">option row</a>:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Panel</strong> — <code>fl-menu[popover]</code>: surface, 1px
        border, 4px radius, 180–280px width. Scrollable menus cap at the
        viewport height and keep their scrollbar.</li>
    <li><strong>Rows</strong> — <code>fl-option</code> buttons (48px, hover
        state layer, full-bleed focus ring) with
        <code>role="menuitem"</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Leading icon</strong> — your SVG or a
        <code>fl-icon</code> placeholder inside the row.</li>
    <li><strong>Meta column</strong> — <code>fl-option__meta</code>:
        right-aligned keyboard commands, secondary color.</li>
    <li><strong>Divider</strong> — a plain <code>&lt;hr&gt;</code> between
        unrelated groups.</li>
    <li><strong>Selection check</strong> — drawn by CSS on
        <code>[aria-checked="true"]</code>; radio rows reserve the leading
        gutter so checked text keeps the column of its unchecked siblings.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-behavior">Behavior</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Markup contract:</strong> the trigger is a
        <code>&lt;button type="button" popovertarget="menu-id"
        aria-haspopup="menu"&gt;</code>; the panel is
        <code>&lt;div class="fl-menu" id="menu-id" popover&gt;</code>.
        Opening is a platform attribute — no <code>data-fl-*</code>
        hook.</li>
    <li><strong>Positioning:</strong> the panel anchors below the trigger's
        left edge and flips when it would clip — right edge first, then
        above the trigger (M2: menus open downward at the top of the
        screen, adapt to the edges elsewhere). flint.js repositions on
        scroll and resize while open. Without flint.js the panel opens
        centered — usable, just unanchored.</li>
    <li><strong>Dismissal:</strong> click outside (light dismiss), Escape,
        selecting an item (author closes with
        <code>menu.hidePopover()</code> in the row's handler), or Tab.
        Focus returns to the trigger on every path.</li>
    <li><strong>Keyboard:</strong> ↑/↓ cycle rows, Home/End jump, Enter/Space
        activate (native button semantics), Escape closes, Tab closes —
        menus are not tab stops (WAI-ARIA).</li>
    <li><strong>Disabled rows:</strong> shown with
        <code>disabled</code>, not removed — M2: display actions as disabled
        when they can only be used sometimes. flint.js skips them in the
        arrow cycle.</li>
    <li><strong>One at a time:</strong> opening a menu closes any other
        open one — native <code>popover</code> light-dismiss
        semantics.</li>
    <li><strong>Animation:</strong> a 150ms fade (M2's transition for menus)
        via <code>@starting-style</code>;
        <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> disables it.</li>
    <li><strong>No-JS fallback (honest):</strong> <code>popovertarget</code>
        opens the panel without flint.js (centered, unanchored). Browsers
        without the Popover API keep menus closed — dialogs and sheets
        (native <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>) cover the critical flows.
        <code>aria-expanded</code> needs flint.js or author code; set it
        yourself in server-rendered apps that skip flint.js.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-accessibility">Accessibility</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Trigger: <code>aria-haspopup="menu"</code> + <code>aria-expanded</code>
        (synced by flint.js) and a real <code>aria-label</code> when
        icon-only ("More options"), never the bare ⋮.</li>
    <li>Panel: <code>role</code> stays native popover — give the panel an
        <code>aria-label</code> ("Note actions") instead of
        <code>role="menu"</code> on the popover element itself; rows carry
        the menu semantics.</li>
    <li>Rows are real <code>&lt;button&gt;s</code>: name, state and disabled
        are announced natively; selection rows use
        <code>aria-checked</code> (<code>menuitemradio</code>), not visual
        state alone.</li>
    <li>Keep row labels short — a menu is scanned, not read (M2:
        scannable).</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-gaps">Known gaps</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Contextual menu (long-press / right-click at tap point):</strong>
        CSS can't gesture and a long-press timer isn't in the JS budget — the
        same honest gap as the mobile tooltip trigger. Right-click menus are
        the browser's own; Flint menus are for consistent triggers.</li>
    <li><strong>Cascading menus:</strong> deliberately not shipped (see
        Types).</li>
    <li><strong>Popover API support:</strong> evergreen browsers only
        (Chrome/Edge 114+, Safari 17+, Firefox 125+); older engines get
        closed menus. This is the one Flint component whose no-JS fallback
        depends on the platform feature, not just on flint.js.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
{% endset %}

